DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ddiirrnnaammee() function is the converse of basename(3); it returns a
     pointer to the parent directory of the pathname pointed to by _p_a_t_h.  Any
     trailing ‘/’ characters are not counted as part of the directory name.

IIMMPPLLEEMMEENNTTAATTIIOONN NNOOTTEESS
     This implementation of ddiirrnnaammee() uses the buffer provided by the caller
     to store the resulting parent directory.  Other vendor implementations
     may return a pointer to internal storage space instead.  The advantage of
     the former approach is that it ensures thread-safety, while also placing
     no upper limit on the supported length of the pathname.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     If _p_a_t_h is a null pointer, the empty string, or contains no ‘/’ charac‐
     ters, ddiirrnnaammee() returns a pointer to the string ".", signifying the cur‐
     rent directory.  Otherwise, it returns a pointer to the parent directory
     of _p_a_t_h.

